---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About | Josh Turknett" currentPage="about" description="Neurologist, researcher, musician, and writer exploring human capability and learning.">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 160px;">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in">
        <h1>Neurologist. Researcher. Author. Musician.</h1>
        <p class="lead" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">It turns out these are all the same thing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Image section - portrait -->
  <section class="image-section">
    <img src="/images/josh-portrait.jpg" alt="Josh Turknett" data-parallax="0.2" style="object-position: center 30%;" />
  </section>

  <!-- Main narrative -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in">
        <p class="lead">
          I'm a neurologist. I spent the first part of my career studying what happens when the brain goes wrong—cognitive disorders, dementia, migraine—and what to do about it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="fade-in fade-in-delay-1">
        <p>
          But the deeper I went, the more I found myself pulled toward the opposite question. Not why brains fail, but how they produce extraordinary capability in the first place—and why we so badly underestimate what every brain is capable of.
        </p>
        <p>
          What struck me is that the questions that matter most about human potential have no field of their own. How do we <strong>build</strong> ability? How do we <strong>access</strong> it when the pressure is on? How do we <strong>sustain</strong> it across a lifetime? Each one gets studied in isolation—by educators, by sports psychologists, by researchers on aging—with no shared science connecting them. And almost none of it starts where it should: with how the brain actually works.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="fade-in fade-in-delay-2">
        <p>
          So I set out to build that field. I founded the <a href="https://performanceneurology.institute" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Institute for Performance Neurology</a> to establish a new discipline—Performance Neurology—devoted to a single question with three parts: how do we build, deploy, and sustain human capability across a lifetime?
        </p>
        <p>
          It's the throughline that connects everything else here. The research, the writing, the teaching, the music—every part of it is Performance Neurology in practice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Research -->
  <section class="section section--gray">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in">
        <h2>Research & Clinical Work</h2>
        <p>
          My research has focused on Alzheimer's disease, cognitive aging, and what protects or accelerates decline. Clinically, I've treated patients with memory disorders, dementia, and complex headache conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The question that runs through all of it: <strong>what does the brain need to stay functional?</strong> The answer keeps coming back to learning. Your brain needs it like your body needs movement.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Brainjo -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split split--reverse">
        <div class="split-image fade-in">
          <img src="/images/concert-wide.jpg" alt="Brainjo" />
        </div>
        <div class="split-content fade-in fade-in-delay-1">
          <h2>Brainjo & Adult Learning</h2>
        <p>
            Brainjo started as an experiment. I wanted to see if learning science could actually help adults who'd given up on music—whether we could systematically remove the obstacles that typically cause people to quit.
          </p>
          <p>
            The experiment grew. Brainjo now reaches thousands of adult learners around the world. And the results keep confirming the same thing: <strong>when adults struggle to learn, the problem is almost never their brain. It's that nobody taught them how.</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Music & Writing -->
  <section class="section section--gray">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in">
        <h2>Music</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          I've played music most of my life—banjo, guitar, fiddle, piano, various stringed things. The music and the neuroscience ended up being the same inquiry from different angles.
        </p>
        <p>
          Understanding how the brain learns made me a better musician. Being a musician gave me a laboratory for testing ideas about learning. The performance work includes several projects: Wandering South, Chillgrass Project, and the Georgia Jays.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="fade-in" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <h2>Writing & Other Work</h2>
        <p>
          I write about the brain, learning, and human capability. The books include <em>Anyone Can Play Music</em>, <em>The Migraine Miracle</em>, and <em>Keto for Migraine</em>. <em>The Genius and the Impostor</em> arrives August 25, 2026, and is <a href="https://geniusandimpostor.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">available for pre-order</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also extend these ideas in more conversational formats through the <a href="https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/better-brain-fitness-a-brainjo-production/id1441139485" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Better Brain Fitness podcast</a> and my newsletter, <a href="https://joshturknett.substack.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brains, Banjos, & Beyond</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
